} (\\alpha + \\beta)A = \\alpha A + \\beta A$ |\n| A8 | For any number $\\alpha$ and any vectors $\\mathbf{u}, \\mathbf{v} \\in \\mathbb{R}^2$, we have $\\color{blue} \\alpha (\\mathbf{u} + \\mathbf{v}) = \\alpha \\mathbf{u} + \\alpha \\mathbf{v}$ | For any number $\\alpha$ and any matrices $A, B \\in \\text{M}_2(\\mathbb{R})$, we have $\\color{blue} \\alpha (A + B) = \\alpha A + \\alpha B$ |" 13194139533439 17592186045585 false] [17592186125838 1064 "Let's also compare some properties of scalar multiplication in $\\mathbb{R}^2$ and $\\text{M}_2(\\mathbb{R}).$ It turns out that the multiplication of a matrix in $\\mathrm{M}_2(\\mathbb{R})$ by a scalar is *very* similar to the multiplication of a vector in $\\mathbb{R}^n$ by a real number. See the table below.\n\n| **** | **$\\mathbb{R}^2$** | **$\\text{M}_2(\\mathbb{R})$** |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| **Invariance Under Multiplication by $1$:** |  |  |\n| A5 | For any vector $\\mathbf{u} \\in \\mathbb{R}^2$, we have $\\color{blue} 1 \\cdot \\mathbf{u} = \\mathbf{u}$ | For any matrix $A \\in \\text{M}_2(\\mathbb{R})$, we have $\\color{blue} 1 \\cdot A = A$ |\n| **Scalar Multiplication is Associative:** |  |  |\n| A6 | For any numbers $\\alpha, \\beta$ and any vector $\\mathbf{u} \\in \\mathbb{R}^2$, we have $\\color{blue} \\alpha (\\beta \\mathbf{u}) = (\\alpha\\beta) \\mathbf{u}$ | For any numbers $\\alpha, \\beta$ and any matrix $A \\in \\text{M}_2(\\mathbb{R})$, we have $\\color{blue} \\alpha (\\beta A) = (\\alpha\\beta) A$ |\n| **Distributive Laws:** |  |  |\n| A7 | For any numbers $\\alpha, \\beta$ and any vector $\\mathbf{u} \\in \\mathbb{R}^2$, we have $\\color{blue} (\\alpha + \\beta)\\mathbf{u} = \\alpha \\mathbf{u} + \\beta \\mathbf{u}$ | For any numbers $\\alpha, \\beta$ and any matrix $A \\in \\text{M}_2(\\mathbb{R})$, we have $\\color{blue} (\\alpha + \\beta)A = \\alpha A + \\beta A$ |\n| A8 | For any number $\\alpha$ and any vectors $\\mathbf{u}, \\mathbf{v} \\in \\mathbb{R}^2$, we have $\\color{blue} \\alpha (\\mathbf{u} + \\mathbf{v}) = \\alpha \\mathbf{u} + \\alpha \\mathbf{v}$ | For any number $\\alpha$ and any matrices $A, B \\in \\text{M}_2(\\mathbb{R})$, we have $\\color{blue} \\alpha (A + B) = \\alpha A + \\alpha B$ |" 13194139533439 17592186045585 true] [17592186125839 1064 "The structure $\\mathbb{R}^n$ is called a **vector space**. However, many other structures possess properties similar to $\\mathbb{R}^n$. These other structures can be called vector spaces too.\n\nIn this lesson, we'll learn about some important properties used to construct vector spaces. We begin by discussing what it means for a set to be **closed** under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nAs an example, consider the set $\\mathrm{M}_2(\\mathbb{R})$ of all $2 \\times 2$ matrices with real entries:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFor this set of matrices, we can define two operations:\n\n- the addition of two matrices, and\n- the multiplication of a matrix by a real number (called a scalar).\n\nNow, notice the following:\n\n- $\\mathrm{M}_2(\\mathbb{R})$ is **closed under addition**. This means that the sum of *any* two matrices from $\\mathrm{M}_2(\\mathbb{R})$ is also a matrix in $\\mathrm{M}_2(\\mathbb{R})$.\n- $\\mathrm{M}_2(\\mathbb{R})$ is **closed under scalar multiplication**. This means that the product of *any* scalar and *any* matrix from $\\mathrm{M}_2(\\mathbb{R})$ is also a matrix in $\\mathrm{M}_2(\\mathbb{R})$.\n\nIdentifying that a set is closed under addition and scalar multiplication is an essential first step in determining whether a set can form a vector space." 13194139533439 17592186045585 false] [17592186125839 1064 "The structure $\\mathbb{R}^n$ is called a **vector space**. However, many other structures possess properties similar to $\\mathbb{R}^n.$ These other structures can be called vector spaces too. \n\nIn this lesson, we'll learn about some important properties used to construct vector spaces. We begin by discussing what it means for a set to be **closed** under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nAs an example, consider the set $\\mathrm{M}_2(\\mathbb{R})$ of all $2 \\times 2$ matrices with real entries:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFor this set of matrices, we can define two operations:\n\n- the addition of two matrices, and\n\n- the multiplication of a matrix by a real number (called a scalar).\n\nIn this context, we often refer to individual elements of [math]\\textrm{M}_2(\\mathbb R)[/math] as **vectors**. This helps us to draw parallels with vectors from [math]\\mathbb R^n.[/math]\n\nNow, notice the following:\n\n- $\\mathrm{M}_2(\\mathbb{R})$ is **closed under addition**. This means that the sum of *any* two matrices from $\\mathrm{M}_2(\\mathbb{R})$ is also a matrix in $\\mathrm{M}_2(\\mathbb{R}).$\n\n- $\\mathrm{M}_2(\\mathbb{R})$ is **closed under scalar multiplication**. This means that the product of *any* scalar and *any* matrix from $\\mathrm{M}_2(\\mathbb{R})$ is also a matrix in $\\mathrm{M}_2(\\mathbb{R}).$\n\nIdentifying that a set is closed under addition and scalar multiplication is an essential first step in determining whether a set can form a vector space." 13194139533439 17592186045585 true] [17592186125858 1064 "So far, we've been using the fact that if $f(x,y)$ is differentiable at the point $(a,b)$, then\n\n$$\nf(x,y) \\approx f(a,b) + \\nabla f(a,b)\\cdot \\langle x-a, y-b \\rangle\n$$\n\nWe can extend this result to higher dimensions.\n\nFor example, if $f(x,y, z)$ is differentiable at the point $(a,b, c)$, then\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z) \\approx f(a,b,c) + \\nabla f(a,b,c)\\cdot \\langle x-a, y-b,z-c \\rangle\n$$\n\nfor all $(x,y,z)$ that lie in some open neighborhood of $(a,b,c)$.\n\nMore generally, if a function $f(\\mathbf x)$ in the variable\n\n$$\n\\mathbf x = (x_1, x_2. \\dots, x_n)\n$$\n\nis differentiable at the point $\\mathbf x_0\\in \\Bbb R^n$, then\n\n$$\nf(\\mathbf x) \\approx f(\\mathbf x_0) + \\nabla f(\\mathbf 