 equal to zero, its discriminant must be less than or equal to zero,\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{D} \\leq 0\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nD\\begin{vmatrix}\\le 0 \\\\ b^{2} - 4ac\\end{vmatrix}\\le 0; (2(u \\cdot v))^{2} - 4 \\cdot ‖u‖^{2} \\cdot ‖v‖^{2}\\begin{vmatrix}\\le 0 \\\\ 4(u \\cdot v)^{2} - 4‖u‖^{2}‖v‖^{2}\\end{vmatrix}\\le 0; (u \\cdot v)^{2}\\begin{vmatrix}\\le ‖u‖^{2}‖v‖^{2} \\\\ \\sqrt{(u \\cdot v)^{2}}\\end{vmatrix}\\le \\sqrt{‖u‖^{2}‖v‖^{2}}; \\mid u \\cdot v \\mid \\mid \\le ‖u‖‖v‖\n$$" 13194139533439 17592186045585 false] [17592186135047 1064 "Now, let's prove the general statement of the Cauchy-Schwarz inequality for vectors $\\mathbf{u}, \\mathbf{v} \\in \\mathbb{R}^n\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\vert \\mathbf{u}\\cdot\\mathbf{v} \\vert \\leq \\Vert \\mathbf{u} \\Vert \\Vert \\mathbf{v} \\Vert\n$$\n\nTo start, let $t\\in\\mathbb{R}$ be any number, and consider the vector $t\\mathbf{u}+\\mathbf{v}.$ Since $\\Vert t\\mathbf{u} + \\mathbf{v} \\Vert \\geq 0,$ using the definition and properties of the dot product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖tu+v{‖}^{2} & \\ge 0 \\\\ (tu+v)\\cdot (tu+v) & \\ge 0 \\\\ {t}^{2}(u\\cdot u)+t(u\\cdot v)+t(v\\cdot u)+(v\\cdot v) & \\ge 0 \\\\ {t}^{2}‖u{‖}^{2}+2t(u\\cdot v)+‖v{‖}^{2} & \\ge 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the last line above, we have a quadratic with the coefficients $a=\\Vert \\mathbf{u} \\Vert^2,$ $b=2\\left( \\mathbf{u}\\cdot\\mathbf{v} \\right),$ and $c=\\Vert \\mathbf{v} \\Vert^2.$\n\nSince the quadratic is greater than or equal to zero, its discriminant must be less than or equal to zero, $\\mathcal{D} \\leq 0.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & \\le 0 \\\\ {b}^{2}-4ac & \\le 0 \\\\ {(2(u\\cdot v))}^{2}-4\\cdot ‖u{‖}^{2}\\cdot ‖v{‖}^{2} & \\le 0 \\\\ 4{(u\\cdot v)}^{2}-4‖u{‖}^{2}‖v{‖}^{2} & \\le 0 \\\\ {(u\\cdot v)}^{2} & \\le ‖u{‖}^{2}‖v{‖}^{2} \\\\ \\sqrt{{(u\\cdot v)}^{2}} & \\le \\sqrt{‖u{‖}^{2}‖v{‖}^{2}} \\\\ |u\\cdot v| & \\le ‖u‖‖v‖.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533439 17592186045585 true] [17592186135079 1064 "In a Euclidean space, the **distance** between the vectors $\\mathbf{x}$ and $\\mathbf{y}$ is the norm of the difference $\\mathbf{x}-\\mathbf{y}$, that is\n\n$$\n\\text{d}(\\mathbf{x}, \\mathbf{y}) = \\Vert \\mathbf{x} - \\mathbf{y} \\Vert\n$$\n\nTo create a formula for the distance, we write the vectors in component form:\n$\\begin{bmatrix}x_{1} \\\\ x_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ x_{n}\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nNow, we can express the norm in terms of the components:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nd(x, y) &= ‖x - y‖ \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{(x_{1} - y_{1})^{2} + (x_{2} - y_{2})^{2} + ⋯ + (x_{n} - y_{n})^{2}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor example, we can use the formula to find the distance between the vectors $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nd(x, y) &= ‖x - y‖ \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{(x_{1} - y_{1})^{2} + (x_{2} - y_{2})^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{(1 - (-5))^{2} + (4 - 1)^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{6^{2} + 3^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{45} \\\\\n&= 3\\sqrt{5}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533439 17592186045585 false] [17592186135079 1064 "In a Euclidean space, the **distance** between the vectors $\\mathbf{x}$ and $\\mathbf{y}$ is the norm of the difference $\\mathbf{x}-\\mathbf{y},$ that is\n\n$$\n\\text{d}(\\mathbf{x}, \\mathbf{y}) = \\Vert \\mathbf{x} - \\mathbf{y} \\Vert.\n$$\n\nTo create a formula for the distance, we write the vectors in component form:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we can express the norm in terms of the components:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}(x,y) & =‖x-y‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{({x}_{1}-{y}_{1})^{2}+({x}_{2}-{y}_{2})^{2}+⋯+({x}_{n}-{y}_{n})^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor example, we can use the formula to find the distance between the vectors $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]$ and $[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}(x,y) & =‖x-y‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{({x}_{1}-{y}_{1})^{2}+({x}_{2}-{y}_{2})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(1-(-5))^{2}+(4-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{6}^{2}+{3}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{45} \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533439 17592186045585 true] [17592186135080 1064 "We can factor a constant out of the norm, provided that we take the absolute value of the constant:\n\n$$\n\\Vert k \\mathbf{x} \\Vert= \\vert k \\vert \\cdot \\Vert \\mathbf{x} \\Vert\n$$\n\nFor example, consider the vector $[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]$ To compute the norm of a multiple of $\\mathbf{x},$ say ${\\color{blue}-2}\\mathbf{x},$ we can factor the absolute value $|{\\color{blue}-2}|$ out of the norm as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖-2x‖ & =|-2|\\cdot ‖x‖ \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\sqrt{{x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\sqrt{{3}^{2}+{4}^{2}} \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\sqrt{9+16} \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\sqrt{25} \\\\  & =2\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** We can verify that we'd get the same result if we multiplied the $\\color{blue}-2$ first, before computing the norm. Multiplying by ${\\color{blue}-2},$ we have\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThen, computing the norm, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖-2x‖ & =\\sqrt{{x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-6)^{2}+(-8)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{36+64} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{100} \\\\  & =10.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533439 17592186045585 true] [17592186135080 1064 "We can factor a constant out of the norm, provided that we take the absolute value of the constant:\n\n$$\n\\Vert k \\mathbf{x} \\Vert= \\vert k \\vert \\cdot \\Vert \\mathbf{x} \\Vert\n$$\n\nFor example, consider the vector $\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}$ To compute the norm of a multiple of $\\mathbf{x}$, say ${\\color{blue}-2}\\mathbf{x}$, we can factor the absolute value $\\mid {\\color{blue}-2} \\mid$ out of the norm as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n‖- 2x‖ &= \\mid - 2 \\mid \\cdot ‖x‖ \\\\\n&= 2 \\cdot \\sqrt{x_{1}^{2} + x_{2}^{2}} \\\\\n&= 2 \\cdot \\sqrt{3^{2} + 4^{2}} \\\\\n&= 2 \\cdot \\sqrt{9 + 16} \\\\\n&= 2 \\cdot \\sqrt{25} \\\\\n&= 2 \\cdot 5 \\\\\n&= 10\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** We can verify that we'd get the same result if we multiplied the $\\color{blue}-2$ first, before computing the norm. Multiplying by ${\\color{blue}-2}$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThen, computing the norm, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n‖- 2x‖ &= \\sqrt{x_{1}^{2} + x_{2}^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{(-6)^{2} + (-8)^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{36 + 64} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{100} \\\\\n&= 10.✓\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533439 17592186045585 false] [17592186135081 1064 "We wish to derive the formula\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_C f(x,y) \\, \\textrm{d}s =\\int_{a}^{b} f(\\mathbf r(t)) \\| \\mathbf r'(t) \\| \\, \\textrm{d}t\n$$\n\nTo do this, we proceed in four steps, as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: We break the integration path $C$ into a series of line segments.\n\nWe choose a finite number of points $P_0$, $P_1$, $\\ldots$, $P_n$ along $C$ and form the polygonal path\n\n$$\n\\overline{P_0P_1} \\cup \\overline{P_1P_2} \\cup \\cdots \\cup \\overline{P_{n-1}P_n}\n$$\n\nOur polygonal path is shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/2d/2d320e6a0f63a823e049203203cbc13c210e80bc98aab5a53bcb4e5299240920.png)\n\n**Step 2**: For each line segment, we approximate the area enclosed by $f(x,y)$ and $C$ between the two endpoints of the segment. To do this, we calculate the area of a rectangle formed by the segment and the surface $z=f(x,y)$.\n\nThe width of each line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\mid \\mid \\overline{P_{k-1}P_k} \\mid \\mid\n$$\n\nIf $P_k$ is at the tip of $\\mathbf r(t_k)$, then by the mean value theorem,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n‖P_{k - 1}P_{k}^(―)‖ &= ‖r^{′}(c_{k})‖(t_{k} - t_{k - 1}) \\\\\n&= ‖r^{′}(c_{k})‖Δt_{k}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor some intermediate point $c_k \\in [t_{k-1}, t_k]$, where\n\n$$\n\\Delta t_k = t_k - t_{k-1}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area formed by the rectangle with a width of $\\big\\| \\overline{P_{k-1}P_k} \\big\\|$ and a height of $f(c_k)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(c_k) \\| \\mathbf r'(c_k)\\|\\Delta t_k\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: We sum the areas of all of our small rectangles, which gives us an *approximation* of our line integral. We get\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_C f(x,y) \\, \\textrm{d}s \\approx \\sum_{k=1}^{n} f(\\mathbf r(c_k)) \\| \\mathbf{r}'(c_k) \\| \\Delta t_k\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: We take the limit as the width of each rectangle tends to zero (or equivalently, as the number of rectangles tends to infinity). This gives us the exact representation of our line integral:\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_C f(x,y) \\, \\textrm{d}s =\\lim_{n\\to\\infty}\\sum_{k=1}^{n} f(\\mathbf r(c_k)) \\| \\mathbf{r}'(c_k) \\| \\Delta t_k = \\int_{a}^{b} f(\\mathbf r(t)) \\| \\mathbf r'(t) \\| \\, \\textrm{d}t\n$$" 13194139533439 17592186045585 false] [17592186135081 1064 "We wish to derive the formula\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_C f(x,y) \\, \\textrm{d}s =\\int_{a}^{b} f(\\mathbf r(t)) \\| \\mathbf r'(t) \\| \\, \\textrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nTo do this, we proceed in four steps, as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: We break the integration path $C$ into a series of line segments.\n\nWe choose a finite number of points $P_0,$ $P_1,$ $\\ldots,$ $P_n$ along $C$ and form the polygonal path\n\n$$\n\\overline{P_0P_1} \\cup \\overline{P_1P_2} \\cup \\cdots \\cup \\overline{P_{n-1}P_n}.\n$$\n\nOur polygonal path is shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/2d/2d320e6a0f63a823e049203203cbc13c210e80bc98aab5a53bcb4e5299240920.png)\n\n**Step 2**: For each line segment, we approximate the area enclosed by $f(x,y)$ and $C$ between the two endpoints of the segment. To do this, we calculate the area of a rectangle formed by the segment and the surface $z=f(x,y).$\n\nThe width of each line segment is given by\n\n$$\n||\\overline{P_{k-1}P_k}|| .\n$$\n\nIf $P_k$ is at the tip of $\\mathbf r(t_k),$ then by the mean value theorem,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖\\overset{―}{{P}_{k-1}{P}_{k}}‖ & =‖{r}^{'}({c}_{k})‖({t}_{k}-{t}_{k-1}) \\\\  & =‖{r}^{'}({c}_{k})‖Δ{t}_{k}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor some intermediate point $c_k \\in [t_{k-1}, t_k],$ where $\\Delta t_k = t_k - t_{k-1}.$\n\nTherefore, the area formed by the rectangle with a width of $\\big\\| \\overline{P_{k-1}P_k} \\big\\|$ and a height of $f(c_k)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(c_k) \\| \\mathbf r'(c_k)\\|\\Delta t_k.\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: We sum the areas of all of our small rectangles, which gives us an *approximation* of our line integral. We get\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_C f(x,y) \\, \\textrm{d}s \\approx \\sum_{k=1}^{n} f(\\mathbf r(c_k)) \\| \\mathbf{r}'(c_k) \\| \\Delta t_k.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: We take the limit as the width of each rectangle tends to zero (or equivalently, as the number of rectangles tends to infinity). This gives us the exact representation of our line integral:\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_C f(x,y) \\, \\textrm{d}s =\\lim_{n\\to\\infty }\\sum_{k=1}^{n} f(\\mathbf r(c_k)) \\| \\mathbf{r}'(c_k) \\| \\Delta t_k = \\int_{a}^{b} f(\\mathbf r(t)) \\| \\mathbf r'(t) \\| \\, \\textrm{d}t\n$$" 13194139533439 17592186045585 true] [17592186135082 1064 "Consider a particle $P$ in the $xy$-plane. Imagine that the motion of $P$ is restricted to the line segment $\\overline{AB}$.\n\nLet $\\mathbf F$ be a *constant* force that acts on $P$, moving it from the point $A$ to the point $B$. A sketch of the situation is shown below.\n\n![](images/79/79f25b863bf7bc9b7a57b5979419c7c8064d223c28d864ea8611d269b000f301.png)\n\nThe **work done** by $\\mathbf F$ in moving $P$ from $A$ to $B$ along this segment is given by\n\n$$\nW = \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf r\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r = \\mathbf b - \\mathbf a\n$$\n\nand $\\mathbf a$ and $\\mathbf b$ are the position vectors of $A$ and $B$, respectively.\n\nThe work done by $\\mathbf F$ is equal to the projection of $\\mathbf F$ in the direction of $\\mathbf r$, multiplied by the length of $\\mathbf r$. It is an essential quantity in physics. However, the formula above is somewhat restrictive:\n\n- Firstly, $\\mathbf F$ must be a constant force. Many forces, such as electric and magnetic forces, vary from point to point.\n- Secondly, the path from $A$ to $B$ must be a line segment. Ideally, we'd like a definition that allows the path from $A$ to $B$ to be a curve.\n\nWe'll now consider a situation where these restrictions are relaxed." 13194139533439 17592186045585 false] [17592186135082 1064 "Consider a particle $P$ in the $xy$-plane. Imagine that the motion of $P$ is restricted to the line segment $\\overline{AB}.$ \n\nLet $\\mathbf F$ be a *constant* force that acts on $P,$ moving it from the point $A$ to the point $B.$ A sketch of the situation is shown below.\n\n![](images/79/79f25b863bf7bc9b7a57b5979419c7c8064d223c28d864ea8611d269b000f301.png)\n\nThe **work done** by $\\mathbf F$ in moving $P$ from $A$ to $B$ along this segment is given by\n\n$$\nW = \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf r\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf r = \\mathbf b - \\mathbf a,$ and $\\mathbf a$ and $\\mathbf b$ are the position vectors of $A$ and $B,$ respectively. \n\nThe work done by $\\mathbf F$ is equal to the projection of $\\mathbf F$ in the direction of $\\mathbf r,$ multiplied by the length of $\\mathbf r.$ It is an essential quantity in physics. However, the formula above is somewhat restrictive:\n\n- Firstly, $\\mathbf F$ must be a constant force. Many forces, such as electric and magnetic forces, vary from point to point.\n\n- Secondly, the path from $A$ to $B$ must be a line segment. Ideally, we'd like a definition that allows the path from $A$ to $B$ to be a curve.\n\nWe'll now consider a situation where these restrictions are relaxed." 13194139533439 17592186045585 true] [17592186135083 1064 "The first type of inner product that we will discuss is the **weighted dot product**.\n\nGiven a sequence of positive real numbers $d_1, d_2, \\ldots d_n > 0$, called \"weights\", the function\n\n$$\n\\langle \\mathbf{x}, \\mathbf{y} \\rangle = \\sum_{i=1}^n d_i x_i y_i\n$$\n\ndefines an inner product on $\\mathbb{R}^n$. This is similar to the dot product, except here, different components are given different weights." 13194139533439 17592186045585 false] [17592186135083 1064 "The first type of inner product that we will discuss is the **weighted dot product**. \n\nGiven a sequence of positive real numbers $d_1, d_2, \\ldots d_n > 0,$ called \"weights\", the function\n\n$$\n\\langle \\mathbf{x}, \\mathbf{y} \\rangle = \\sum_{i=1}^n d_i x_i y_i\n$$\n\ndefines an inner product on $\\mathbb{R}^n.$ This is similar to the dot product, except here, different components are given different weights." 13194139533439 17592186045585 true] [17592186135084 1064 "The next inner product that we will discuss concerns inner products on polynomials.\n\nGiven a sequence of distinct real numbers ![](images/52/52cae406fa2952a80a1bab7e02d5667b3acda56e4b2456a3dcb6600a91105482.svg) the function\n![](images/d1/d1f9dd085bdf1f3d49f99a26d17d75a4e1bd1ee413fff06bfff4198e4e1ee59d.svg)\ndefines an inner product on ![](images/35/353273654347dcd414aa60782023eff4d676ea7f598a8d2f9c923ee6c4cba811.svg) the vector space of polynomials of degree at most ![](images/c2/c2475d72e6ec36042292de6c0160930174172b2386148d823a70e59fddfb93c2.svg)\n\nLet's consider a few examples:\n\n- The following function defines an inner product on ![](images/ba/ba47d908e29aa7475933ff0536d610abe1e3752dd165c8cf6f655f83ae48156b.svg)\n![](images/24/24d15f48f890f3bff207460d175db7d16b35fd21a05e1dc3d1103cde1453c531.svg)\n\n- The following function defines an inner product on ![](images/50/507c62e239664ab41c74f85c0e905c0a84d3da5183991232db789ce8a9d385f1.svg)\n![](images/e3/e3003acc01323f7ff829cab929cec80c6d8a4d340f66642db172f0926533c9c5.svg)\n\n- The following function defines an inner product on ![](images/c5/c5c6bb73a744c88af37d2cbce6ac9e27957fc77dbcddb4ec6fb04fb02ac1bbc2.svg)\n![](images/5f/5f66a28d825609fe3e9c6dca065cb3f96e6fe0fafccd56ff87d7608fa7cf35af.svg)\n\nNotice that this inner product on ![](images/29/297749fafabb3d9d253c58491c364d113f5579eacda31a07a8b8c792cc9d7777.svg) requires summing ![](images/9f/9fb9bc4dd378568f6cbe7adaa9000da66d51c5eda878e60aea5131718d33ec3d.svg) terms. This is essential to ensure positive definiteness. For example, the function\n![](images/e3/e3003acc01323f7ff829cab929cec80c6d8a4d340f66642db172f0926533c9c5.svg)\nis *not* an inner product on ![](images/68/686b2d83c1abe018b786ecc5f7c624a4932fda35b57d09ca5385a41f8a3bb233.svg) since there exist nonzero polynomials ![](images/aa/aa374f18ad64a3743718af3bbcef010a756b25dae566ccb9413a790a97b72067.svg) such that ![](images/d1/d138796b5b181d78264c70ef5c9b68db4dba9c6603612cca303c87e09924ec2d.svg) which violates the condition ![](images/63/631ddd9e222592497d44379cd2d6b6aac118ff1a0e4c6587347473112aae26c5.svg)\n\nIn general, a nonzero polynomial of degree at most ![](images/fb/fb2a8560bbc7122a4dd4f1a0bb9862d4368a63f4b03968884a3a60e7423a852c.svg) can have at most ![](images/fb/fb2a8560bbc7122a4dd4f1a0bb9862d4368a63f4b03968884a3a60e7423a852c.svg) distinct roots, so vanishing at ![](images/9f/9fb9bc4dd378568f6cbe7adaa9000da66d51c5eda878e60aea5131718d33ec3d.svg) distinct points forces the polynomial to be zero." 13194139533439 17592186045585 true] [17592186135084 1064 "The next inner product that we will discuss concerns inner products on polynomials.\n\nGiven a sequence of distinct real numbers $t_1, t_2, \\ldots, t_{n+1}$, the function\n\n$$\n\\langle x(t), y(t) \\rangle = \\sum_{i=1}^{n+1} x(t_i) y(t_i)\n$$\n\ndefines an inner product on $\\mathbb{R}_n[t]$, the vector space of polynomials of degree at most $n$.\n\nLet's consider a few examples:\n\n- The following function defines an inner product on $\\mathbb{R}_1[t]$:\n$\\langle p(x), q(x)\\rangle = p(0)q(0) + p(1)q(1)$\n\n- The following function defines an inner product on $\\mathbb{R}_2[t]$:\n$\\langle p(x), q(x)\\rangle = p(-1)q(-1) + p(0)q(0) + p(1)q(1)$\n- The following function defines an inner product on $\\mathbb{R}_3[t]$:\n$\\langle p(x), q(x)\\rangle = p(-1)q(-1) + p(0)q(0) + p(1)q(1) + p(2)q(2)$\n\nNotice that this inner product on $\\mathbb R_n[t]$ requires summing $n+1$ terms. This is essential to ensure positive definiteness. For example, the function\n\n$$\n\\langle p(x), q(x)\\rangle = p(-1)q(-1) + p(0)q(0) + p(1)q(1)\n$$\n\nis *not* an inner product on $\\mathbb R_3[t]$, since there exist nonzero polynomials $f(x)\\in\\mathbb R_3[t]$ such that\n\n$$\nf(-1) = f(0) = f(1) = 0\n$$\n\nwhich violates the condition $\\langle f(x), f(x) \\rangle > 0$." 13194139533439 17592186045585 false] [17592186135085 1064 "Our final inner product is a generalization of what we saw in the previous example. The function\n\n$$\n\\langle x(t), y(t) \\rangle = \\displaystyle\\int_{a}^{b} x(t) y(t) \\, \\textrm{d}t\n$$\n\ndefines an inner product on $\\mathcal{C}[a,b]$, the vector space of continuous functions on $[a,b]$.\n\nHere, instead of finding the product of the functions at some discrete points and summing them, we sum (integrate) the product of the functions over the whole interval $[a,b]$." 13194139533439 17592186045585 false] [17592186135085 1064 "Our final inner product is a generalization of what we saw in the previous example. The function\n\n$$\n\\langle x(t), y(t) \\rangle = \\displaystyle\\int_{a}^{b} x(t) y(t) \\, \\textrm{d}t\n$$\n\ndefines an inner product on $\\mathcal{C}[a,b],$ the vector space of continuous functions on $[a,b].$ \n\nHere, instead of finding the product of the functions at some discrete points and summing them, we sum (integrate) the product of the functions over the whole interval $[a,b].$" 13194139533439 17592186045585 true] [17592186135086 1064 "A **line integral** $\\displaystyle\\int\\limits_C f(x, y) \\, \\textrm{d}s$ is similar to a definite integral, but for a function of multiple variables along a general path $C$.\n\nTo understand the intuition behind line integrals, remember that the definite integral $\\displaystyle\\int_a^b f(x)\\,\\textrm{d}x$ can be interpreted as the (signed) area bounded between the function $y=f(x)$ and the $x$-axis between $x=a$ and $x=b$, as shown below.\n\n![](images/93/93ce31caadb9d753a13acc2f25b898738aa67b70f1374816fccfc0cf3be21b7a.png)\n\nIntuitively, we can think of the integral as the result of summing the areas of many small rectangles that we accumulate as we move along the $x$-axis on the **path** from $x=a$ to $x=b$.\n\nWe can also draw paths as general curves in the plane. For example, consider the path $C$, given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t) = \\langle 2\\cos t, 2\\sin t \\rangle, \\qquad t\\in \\left[ 0, \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right]\n$$\n\nThis path represents a quarter circle of radius $2$ traversed counterclockwise from $(2,0)$ to $(0,2)$ in the first quadrant:\n\n![](images/6d/6d933c280ebb530aefaadc1b5ef927e39603635960348255045c138ee9bab366.png)\n\nLet\n\n$$\nf(x,y)= 3 - \\sin (2xy)\n$$\n\nIf we sum the areas of the thin rectangles that are formed by the curve $C$ and the surface $z=f(x,y)$ as we move along the curve $C$ from $(2,0)$ to $(0,2)$, we get a *signed area,* as shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/4c/4c3b973883d1943eebc47d168f6c2fcf00cf50702ce9afdfec7d5031735bbbff.png)\n\nThis signed area is equal to the line integral $\\displaystyle\\int\\limits_C f(x, y) \\, \\textrm{d}s$.\n\nFinally, we sometimes call $\\displaystyle\\int\\limits_C f(x, y) \\, \\textrm{d}s$ as the line integral of $f$ **with respect to arc length.** Other types of line integral are possible, as we'll see soon." 13194139533439 17592186045585 false] [17592186135086 1064 "A **line integral** $\\displaystyle\\int\\limits_C f(x, y) \\, \\textrm{d}s$ is similar to a definite integral, but for a function of multiple variables along a general path $C.$\n\nTo understand the intuition behind line integrals, remember that the definite integral $\\displaystyle\\int_a^b f(x)\\,\\textrm{d}x$ can be interpreted as the (signed) area bounded between the function $y=f(x)$ and the $x$-axis between $x=a$ and $x=b,$ as shown below. \n\n![](images/93/93ce31caadb9d753a13acc2f25b898738aa67b70f1374816fccfc0cf3be21b7a.png)\n\nIntuitively, we can think of the integral as the result of summing the areas of many small rectangles that we accumulate as we move along the $x$-axis on the **path** from $x=a$ to $x=b.$\n\nWe can also draw paths as general curves in the plane. For example, consider the path $C,$ given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t) = \\langle 2\\cos t, 2\\sin t \\rangle, \\qquad t\\in \\left[ 0, \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right].\n$$\n\nThis path represents a quarter circle of radius $2$ traversed counterclockwise from $(2,0)$ to $(0,2)$ in the first quadrant:\n\n![](images/6d/6d933c280ebb530aefaadc1b5ef927e39603635960348255045c138ee9bab366.png)\n\nLet $f(x,y)= 3 - \\sin (2xy) .$ If we sum the areas of the thin rectangles that are formed by the curve $C$ and the surface $z=f(x,y)$ as we move along the curve $C$ from $(2,0)$ to $(0,2),$ we get a *signed area,* as shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/4c/4c3b973883d1943eebc47d168f6c2fcf00cf50702ce9afdfec7d5031735bbbff.png)\n\nThis signed area is equal to the line integral $\\displaystyle\\int\\limits_C f(x, y) \\, \\textrm{d}s.$\n\nFinally, we sometimes call $\\displaystyle\\int\\limits_C f(x, y) \\, \\textrm{d}s$ the line integral of $f$ **with respect to arc length.** Other types of line integral are possible, as we'll see soon." 13194139533439 17592186045585 true] [17592186135087 1064 "Let $C$ be a \"smooth\" curve, defined parametrically as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r} = \\mathbf{r}(t), \\qquad t \\in (a,b)\n$$\n\nNow let $f(x,y)$ be a continuous function that is defined everywhere on $C$. The **line integral** of $f$ over $C$, denoted\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\int\\limits_C f(x, y) \\, \\textrm{d}s\n$$\n\ncan be calculated using the formula\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_C f(x, y) \\, \\textrm{d}s = \\int\\limits_a^b f(\\mathbf r(t)) \\, \\| \\mathbf r'(t) \\| \\, \\textrm{d}t\n$$\n\nThe line integral formula extends to functions of $n$ variables. For example, for functions of three variables, we can use the formula\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_C f(x, y,z) \\, \\textrm{d}s = \\int\\limits_a^b f(\\mathbf r(t)) \\, \\| \\mathbf r'(t) \\| \\, \\textrm{d}t\n$$\n\nWe'll go through the details of where this formula comes from at the end of the lesson. But for now, let's get some practice at computing line integrals." 13194139533439 17592186045585 false] [17592186135087 1064 "Let $C$ be a \"smooth\" curve, defined parametrically as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r} = \\mathbf{r}(t), \\qquad t \\in (a,b).\n$$\n\nNow let $f(x,y)$ be a continuous function that is defined everywhere on $C.$ The **line integral** of $f$ over $C,$ denoted \n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\int\\limits_C f(x, y) \\, \\textrm{d}s,\n$$\n\ncan be calculated using the formula\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_C f(x, y) \\, \\textrm{d}s  = \\int\\limits_a^b  f(\\mathbf r(t)) \\, \\| \\mathbf r'(t) \\| \\, \\textrm{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe line integral formula extends to functions of $n$ variables. For example, for functions of three variables, we can use the formula\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_C f(x, y,z) \\, \\textrm{d}s  = \\int\\limits_a^b  f(\\mathbf r(t)) \\, \\| \\mathbf r'(t) \\| \\, \\textrm{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nWe'll go through the details of where this formula comes from at the end of the lesson. But for now, let's get some practice at computing line integrals." 13194139533439 17592186045585 true] [17592186135088 1064 "How do we represent the double integral of a function over a non-rectangular region as a repeated integral?\n\nSpecifically, let's consider\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\iint\\limits_D (x-2y) \\, \\textrm d A, %= \\int_a^b \\int_c^d (x-2y) \\,\\textrm d y\\, \\textrm d x\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is the finite region enclosed by the lines $x=1$, $x=3$, $y = x$ and\n\n$$\ny = \\dfrac12(x+4)\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/af/af0a1a74df8aec0d5189dc2402fc1840c25d4cd94d8861fa06110cae7b449843.png)\n\nFirst, notice that $D$ is a *type I* region.\nTo determine the lower and upper functions, we draw a vertical arrow through the region.\n\n![](images/39/39629b33e25aed05bf9a5ca36180f984e7ff8f863c6966d47f60483d672a8af4.png)\n\nSo, the region $D$ can be defined as\n\n$$\nD = \\left\\{(x,y) \\:: \\: {\\color{red}1} \\leq x \\leq {\\color{blue}3}, \\quad {\\color{red}x} \\leq y \\leq {\\color{blue}\\dfrac12(x+4)} \\right\\}\n$$\n\nNow, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∬_(D)(x - 2y)dA &= \\int_((left limit))^((right limit))[\\int_((lower function))^((upper function))(x - 2y)dy]dx \\\\\n&= \\int_{1}^{3}[\\int_{x}^{(x + 4)/2}(x - 2y)dy]dx\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAnd that's our answer! Notice that, when we have a type I region,\n\n- the limits for $y$ are functions of $x$, and\n- the limits for $x$ are constants." 13194139533439 17592186045585 false] [17592186135088 1064 "How do we represent the double integral of a function over a non-rectangular region as a repeated integral? \n\nSpecifically, let's consider\n![](images/3a/3ac99dd8336c3a877fbf6ba63a3074a490b85c8a350adad00da423412dd60ee3.svg)\n\nwhere ![](images/59/5914524b1d3db63c66998b75ca1d00a5dc4e294d2a8f10fe384d2b2ebf2af38e.svg)  is the finite region enclosed by the lines ![](images/df/df9822243c11ec9f0fabca5cc578976d0bdea2c521b4f61964f5f3850a970504.svg), ![](images/1f/1fb9f2f73a09599931892c1dee7629975b4d851e5dd6f0d07b7c641bbf17bf54.svg), ![](images/23/238e977be23f692486c8bf68f6b40255435b3d1796cc50d260a014a8c670be7b.svg) and ![](images/b5/b5489c125683eb29c2cababe2e1b7094d6a16b89d09970c42af649df70d045e4.svg) as shown below.\n\n![](images/af/af0a1a74df8aec0d5189dc2402fc1840c25d4cd94d8861fa06110cae7b449843.png)\n\nFirst, notice that ![](images/59/5914524b1d3db63c66998b75ca1d00a5dc4e294d2a8f10fe384d2b2ebf2af38e.svg) is a *type I* region. \nTo determine the lower and upper functions, we draw a vertical arrow through the region.\n\n![](images/39/39629b33e25aed05bf9a5ca36180f984e7ff8f863c6966d47f60483d672a8af4.png)\n\nSo, the region ![](images/59/5914524b1d3db63c66998b75ca1d00a5dc4e294d2a8f10fe384d2b2ebf2af38e.svg) can be defined as \n\n![](images/78/78cf2ea7f960497f7cc9beea602bf6ca0e82b74491f9f56cce76130d965d4a64.svg)\n\nNow, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n![](images/3c/3c423b0d423203621ab89258a6fff4cea190279b4dcc7170c8a7070bf1f85e24.svg)\n\nAnd that's our answer! Notice that, when we have a type I region, \n\n- the limits for ![](images/1d/1d9b846cface9f9348e4081a0dd87dd254bf26663c12aa0d1a15aa24d2393261.svg) are functions of ![](images/5a/5aac2c81021f3f9e468e7840c5f95ece35c10b894bdae19ec1d247bdeb47ac6f.svg)\n\n- the limits for ![](images/fc/fcc5df999e3b49fae1685ee0ab5bd03709804e1dd49f2ee722c4269eb0f5506d.svg) are constants, and\n\n- we integrate over the variable ![](images/1d/1d9b846cface9f9348e4081a0dd87dd254bf26663c12aa0d1a15aa24d2393261.svg) before integrating over the variable ![](images/cf/cff7b257e3c71ec70dc7cd273bdd1cced4254993a43cffbfc338460c21bc7319.svg)" 13194139533439 17592186045585 true] [17592186135089 1064 "Suppose that we want to evaluate the repeated integral\n\n$$\n\\int_{0}^{1}\\int_{x}^{2x} (x-2y) \\ \\mathrm{d}  y \\ \\mathrm{d}  x.\n$$\n\nNotice that the domain of integration is a type I region. \n\nTo compute this integral, we must bear the following important points in mind.\n\n- We start with the inner integral, as usual.\n\n- When integrating with respect to $y,$ we treat $x$ as though it were a constant.\n\n- The limits of integration for the inner integral are functions of $x.$ Once again, we treat $x$ as though it were a constant and substitute the integration limits as usual. After this step, there should be no more $y$'s in our integral.\n\n- Finally, we compute the outer integral.\n\nLet's apply these steps to solve the given problem.\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $y,$ treating $x$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{x}^{2x}(x-2y)\\text{ }dy]\\,dx & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[xy-{y}^{2}]_{x}^{2x}\\text{ }dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[x\\cdot (2x)-{(2x)}^{2}]-[x\\cdot (x)-(x)^{2}]dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[2{x}^{2}-4{x}^{2}]-[{x}^{2}-{x}^{2}]dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}-2{x}^{2}\\,dx \\\\  & =-2{\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{2}\\,dx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2{\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{2}\\,dx & =-2{[\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}{[{x}^{3}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}[{1}^{3}-{0}^{3}] \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533439 17592186045585 true] [17592186135089 1064 "Suppose that we want to evaluate the repeated integral\n\n$$\n\\int_{0}^{1}\\int_{x}^{2x} (x-2y) \\ \\mathrm{d} y \\ \\mathrm{d} x\n$$\n\nNotice that the domain of integration is a type I region.\n\nTo compute this integral, we must bear the following important points in mind.\n\n- We start with the inner integral, as usual.\n- When integrating with respect to $y$, we treat $x$ as though it were a constant.\n- The limits of integration for the inner integral are functions of $x$. Once again, we treat $x$ as though it were a constant and substitute the integration limits as usual. After this step, there should be no more $y$'s in our integral.\n- Finally, we compute the outer integral.\n\nLet's apply these steps to solve the given problem.\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $y$, treating $x$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_{0}^{1}[\\int_{x}^{2x}(x - 2y)dy]dx &= \\int_{0}^{1}[xy - y^{2}]_{x}^{2x}dx \\\\\n&= \\int_{0}^{1}[x \\cdot (2x) - (2x)^{2}] - [x \\cdot (x) - (x)^{2}]dx \\\\\n&= \\int_{0}^{1}[2x^{2} - 4x^{2}] - [x^{2} - x^{2}]dx \\\\\n&= \\int_{0}^{1} - 2x^{2}dx \\\\\n&=-2\\int_{0}^{1}x^{2}dx\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-2\\int_{0}^{1}x^{2}dx &= -2[\\frac{x^{3}}{3}]_{0}^{1} \\\\\n&=-\\frac{2}{3}[x^{3}]_{0}^{1} \\\\\n&=-\\frac{2}{3}[1^{3} - 0^{3}] \\\\\n&=-\\frac{2}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533439 17592186045585 false] [17592186135091 1064 "How do we represent a double integral over a type II region as a repeated integral?\n\nSpecifically, let's consider\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\iint\\limits_D (x+2y) \\, \\textrm d A, %= \\int_a^b \\int_c^d (x+2y) \\,\\textrm d y\\, \\textrm d x\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is the finite region bounded by the lines $x=0$, $y=0, y=1$ and the curve $x=e^y$, as shown below.\n\n![](images/09/09fa628c26853d48f3775945e856763b5a996c431785ecf4194c879ee881aa78.png)\n\nFirst, notice that $D$ is indeed a type II region.\nTo determine the right and left functions, we draw a horizontal arrow through the region, as usual.\n\n![](images/47/47fffd047fe12aacfbcc98fa34750e7afcb32dd06618f2553c65432828fff328.png)\n\nHence, the region $D$ can be defined as\n\n$$\nD = \\left\\{(x,y) \\:: \\: {\\color{red}0} \\leq y \\leq {\\color{blue}1}, \\quad {\\color{red}0} \\leq x \\leq {\\color{blue}e^y} \\right\\}\n$$\n\nNow, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∬_(D)(x + 2y)dA &= \\int_((lower limit))^((upper limit))[\\int_((left function))^((right function))(x + 2y)dx]dy \\\\\n&= \\int_{0}^{1}[\\int_{0}^{e^{y}}(x + 2y)dx]dy\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAnd that's our answer! Notice that when we have a type II region,\n\n- the limits for $x$ are functions of $y$, and\n- the limits for $y$ are constants." 13194139533439 17592186045585 false] [17592186135091 1064 "How do we represent a double integral over a type II region as a repeated integral? \n\nSpecifically, let's consider\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\iint\\limits_D (x+2y) \\, \\textrm d A, \n%= \\int_a^b \\int_c^d (x+2y) \\,\\textrm d y\\, \\textrm d x,\n$$\n\nwhere $D$  is the finite region bounded by the lines $x=0,$ $y=0, y=1$ and the curve $x=e^y,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/09/09fa628c26853d48f3775945e856763b5a996c431785ecf4194c879ee881aa78.png)\n\nFirst, notice that $D$ is indeed a type II region. \nTo determine the right and left functions, we draw a horizontal arrow through the region, as usual.\n\n![](images/47/47fffd047fe12aacfbcc98fa34750e7afcb32dd06618f2553c65432828fff328.png)\n\nHence, the region $D$ can be defined as 