angle \\sin(kx),\\sin(nx)\\rangle=0$ whenever $k\\ne n,$ so\n\n$$\n\\langle f(x),\\sin(nx)\\rangle=b_n\\langle \\sin(nx),\\sin(nx)\\rangle.\n$$\n\nSince $\\displaystyle \\langle \\sin(nx),\\sin(nx)\\rangle=\\int_{-\\pi}^{\\pi} \\sin^2(nx)\\,\\textrm{d}x=\\pi,$ we obtain\n\n$$\nb_n=\\dfrac{\\langle f(x),\\sin(nx)\\rangle}{\\langle \\sin(nx),\\sin(nx)\\rangle}\n=\\dfrac{1}{\\pi}\\int_{-\\pi}^{\\pi} f(x)\\sin(nx)\\,\\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nPutting these together, the Fourier coefficients are the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{0} & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ {a}_{n} & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }f(x)\\operatorname{cos}(nx)\\,\\text{d}x,\\,n\\ge 1 \\\\ {b}_{n} & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }f(x)\\operatorname{sin}(nx)\\,\\text{d}x,\\,n\\ge 1\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533439 17592186045585 true] [17592186168058 1064 "Recall that a function $f(x)$ is *$p$-periodic* if\n\n$$\nf(x+p)=f(x)\n$$\n\nfor all $x\\in\\mathbb{R}$.\n\nNow, if $F(x)$ is defined on an interval $x \\in [a,b)$ of length $p$, then its **$p$-periodic extension** $f$ is defined by the two properties:\n\n- $f(x+p)=f(x)$ for all $x \\in \\mathbb{R}$\n- $f(x)=F(x)$ for all $x \\in [a,b)$.\n\nFor example, let $F(x) = -x$ be defined on $x\\in [0,2)$. Now, suppose $f(x)$ is the $2$-periodic extension of $F(x)$ from $x\\in [0,2)$ to $x \\in \\mathbb{R}$. The graphs of $y=F(x)$ and $y=f(x)$ are shown below.\n\n![](images/fe/fe66db8a73acee21bbd5f7c95d7598b448cb678408d2f47754ee4c30aefd5b14.png)\n\n**Watch out!** We should always be aware of the difference between $F(x)$ and its extension $f(x)$. For instance, consider the function\n\n$$\nF(x)=2\\sin(x)\n$$\n\ndefined on $x \\in [0,\\pi)$ and its $\\pi$-periodic extension $f(x)$. The graphs of $y=F(x)$ and $y=f(x)$ are shown below.\n\n![](images/b1/b1299bb593554a930a12870d39c74aa8d23ea97027b9e9e253e8fda6b592e42a.png)\n\nNotice that $f(x)$ is not just $2\\sin(x)$! But it would coincide with $2\\sin(x